INT. LAUNDROMAT - NIGHT

Twenty-four-hour laundromat on a dead corner. Fluorescent tubes buzz. Rows of dryers tumble in the dark. A row of orange plastic chairs faces a window onto the empty street.

LUCINDA "LUCY" REYES, 30s, scrubs under a coat, sits slumped in the nearest chair watching a dryer turn. Beside her, her younger brother TEO REYES, 22, thin, new haircut, holds a plastic basket of folded shirts on his lap like a shield. At the change machine, feeding quarters one at a time, stands their mother ADELA REYES, 60s, in a church dress and sneakers.

The coins ring in the dispenser.

ADELA
Teo. Come help me.

TEO
I'm busy.

ADELA
With what?

TEO
Sitting.

LUCY
He's being a man.

TEO
Lucy.

LUCY
Sorry. Being a grown-up.

Adela collects the quarters in her cupped hands and carries them over.

ADELA
Two loads. Forty minutes.

LUCY
Mom, it's midnight.

ADELA
So? We have forty minutes.

She sits between them. She sets the quarters on her knee and counts them. Teo sets down the basket. Lucy tilts her head back against the wall.

TEO
I got the job.

Adela's fingers stop.

LUCY
What job?

TEO
The one in Tucson.

LUCY
(sitting up)
Tucson?

TEO
I leave Friday.

Silence. The dryers hum. Adela resumes counting.

ADELA
That's good.

TEO
You're not mad?

ADELA
Why would I be?

TEO
Because you said--

ADELA
I said a lot of things.

LUCY
Teo, Mom's crying.

ADELA
I'm not crying.

LUCY
You are.

ADELA
It's the lint.

Teo takes the quarters from his mother's knee and puts them in her palm, and she closes her fingers around his hand.
